    "https://hartford.peopleadmin.com/postings/8043": {
      "title": "Clinical/Applied Teaching Assistant Professor of Technical Theatre (2027 2028)",
      "description": "University of Hartford Employment Site | Clinical/Applied Teaching Assistant Professor of Technical Theatre (2027 2028) Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Clinical/Applied Teaching Assistant Professor of Technical Theatre (2027 2028) Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Within the required Statement on Inclusive Excellence and Belonging, candidates should include an explanation of how their teaching practice meets the needs of the diverse population of students at the University of Hartford, focusing particularly on pedagogical approaches that support student success. If relevant, candidates should also comment on research, scholarship, practice, or creative activity that will contribute to the inclusive excellence goals to which the University of Hartford is committed. It is recommended that candidates approach this statement thoughtfully and use specific examples to illustrate their Statement on Inclusive Excellence and Belonging. In your cover letter, please relate your experience to all Qualifications of the position in addition to highlighting what unique skills you would bring to The Hartt School. Posting Details Position Information Job Title Clinical/Applied Teaching Assistant Professor of Technical Theatre (2027 2028) Rank Assistant Professor Salary Range $68,000 - $74,000 Tenure Information Clinical/Applied Job Description The Hartt School, a conservatory of music, dance, and theatre, at the University of Hartford is seeking a Clinical Applied Teaching Assistant Professor of Technical Theater beginning August 2027. Candidates are expected to teach technical theater to undergraduate students in the Technical Theater, Bachelor of Fine Arts program. Responsibilities Teach technical theater to undergraduate students in the Technical Theater, Bachelor of Fine Arts program. Mentor technical theater students through classroom, laboratory, and production experiences. Maintain a safe, professional, and inclusive production environment while modeling current industry best practices in technical theater. Collaborate with faculty and production staff to support an active season of mainstage and studio productions. Prepare students for careers in the professional theatre industry through intensive hands-on training and production experiences. Maintain sustained excellence and innovation in the candidate’s clinical/applied practice and service to academic, professional, or Hartford area communities. Engage in regular department service, including advising and student recruitment and retention. Demonstrate a commitment to inclusive excellence, which the University defines as an unwavering commitment to fostering a vibrant campus community where every student, staff member, and faculty colleague feels deeply rooted, empowered to flourish, and inspired to achieve their highest potential. The duration of the initial Clinical/Applied Teaching contract is one or two years, and subsequent, longer-term contracts are based on review of performance and the continuing need for the position. Required Qualifications Required Qualifications: Master of Fine Arts ( MFA ) in Technical Direction, Theatre Technology, Scenic Design, Lighting Design, or significant national and/or international professional experience, at the time of appointment. Demonstrated success teaching and mentoring undergraduate students and leading student production crews. Experience supervising production processes. Demonstrated commitment to production safety and best practices. Proficiency using and teaching production-related, industry-standard software, for example, Vectorworks, AutoCAD, and Show Control. Preferred Qualifications: Expertise in one or more areas including scenic construction, lighting, audio, rigging, projections, drafting, or technical direction. Excellent interpersonal and organizational skills with the ability to manage multiple, overlapping project deadlines. Experience recruiting, supporting, and retaining students. Cross-disciplinary collaboration interests (e.g., working with music, dance, or digital arts programs) Commitment to Inclusive Excellence and Belonging: Candidate shows evidence of fostering an inclusive, equitable, just, and caring community where the unique identities of each person are fully appreciated, respected, and celebrated. Candidate demonstrates values that align with diverse intellectual perspectives. Candidate is capable of cultivating a campus community of respect and belonging. Preferred Qualifications Statement on Inclusive Excellence and Teaching Philosophy Statement of Inclusive Excellence As an institution of higher learning, the University of Hartford strives to be more than merely a mirror of the larger society; it should foster learning and encourage the personal growth of students, faculty, and staff in an environment that promotes and celebrates diversity in all of its forms regardless of identifying factors including but not limited to race, sex, age, religion, national origin, socioeconomic status, physical and learning differences, sexual orientation, gender or gender identity. Accordingly, our commitment is to: · Foster an inclusive, equitable, just, and caring community where the unique identities of each person are fully appreciated, respected, and celebrated · Engage in civil discourse, that values diverse intellectual perspectives · Cultivate a campus community reflective of the intersectionality of the larger society while maintaining an environment of safety and respect where all members experience a sense of belonging · Institutionally, stand against acts of hate, intimidation, discrimination, bigotry, violence, and harassment of any kind · Uphold the values of the institution, so that all our members are treated with dignity and respect Our commitment to Inclusive Excellence and Belonging is a strength that results in a vibrant and more engaged campus culture. Teaching Philosophy In the teaching philosophy statement, please share your thoughts and experiences in using active learning strategies, high-impact practices, and evidence-based pedagogical practices, as well as your methods for engaging students and fostering student belonging in the classroom. Please also include strategies for recruiting and retaining quality students and pedagogical methods for technical theater students. University Information Students at the University of Hartford find success in a learning environment that both challenges and mentors them. Our academic mission is to engage students in acquiring the knowledge, skills, and values necessary to thrive in, and contribute to, a pluralistic, complex world. At the University of Hartford, we are committed to community. We are an academic community that values integrity, curiosity, creativity, excellence, responsibility, and accomplishment. Enriched by our engagement with, and understanding of, one another, we take pride in our shared traditions and experiences. We are dedicated to building a culture that respects all of its members and celebrates their contributions as we work together to strengthen our community. The full text of our academic mission, vision, and values can be seen at https:// www.hartford.edu . Opportunities for faculty development and support can be found at https://www.hartford.edu/about/campus-leadership/office-provost/faculty-development/ and a listing of Affinity Networks can be found at https://www.hartford.edu/about/diversity-inclusion/affinity-groups.aspx College Information Hartt Theatre The Hartt School offers accredited undergraduate and graduate programs in music, dance, and theatre. The Hartt School is a school of performing arts within a mid-size university setting. Hartt students benefit from a diverse array of master classes, artist residencies, internships, and partnerships with leading professional performing arts organizations that help them achieve mastery in their discipline, find personal fulfillment, and transition successfully from college to career. The Hartt School has a total enrollment of 460 undergraduate and 120 graduate students served by 43 full-time and 133 part-time faculty with a 12:1 faculty to student ratio. The Theatre Division at Hartt offers rigorous, pre-professional, specialized training in Music Theatre, Actor Training, Technical Theater, and Stage Management, providing students with extensive hands-on production experience through a robust season of mainstage and studio productions. Students collaborate across disciplines with theatre, dance, and music artists while developing the professional skills necessary for successful careers in the performing arts. The program benefits from its proximity to New York City and serves approximately 109 Music Theatre majors, 78 Actor Training majors, nine Stage Management students, and 10 Technical Theater students with six full-time faculty, four full-time staff, and 50 part-time faculty and guest artists. It offers BFA degrees in Actor Training, Music Theatre, Stage Management, and Technical Theater. The yearly season consists of up to eight Main Stage productions and eight Studio Projects which are directed, music directed, and/or choreographed by faculty and guest artists. The Theatre Division is housed in the Handel Performing Arts Center that is shared with the Dance Division and contains two Black Box spaces. Additional proscenium and modified thrust spaces are located on the nearby main campus. Our BFA programs have a national reputation, with alumni currently or recently working on Broadway in Aladdin , Music Man , MJ , Funny Girl , Leopoldstadt , Some Like it Hot , Harry Potter and the Cursed Child ; in London’s West End in Clueless and MJ , and in numerous Regional and Summer Stock houses across the U.S. The University of Hartford is fully accredited by the National Association of Schools of Theatre. For more information about Hartt Theatre, please visit: https://www.hartford.edu/academics/schools-colleges/hartt/academics/theatre/default.aspx Posting Detail Information Posting Number F225P Open Date 08/16/2027 Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Special Instructions to Applicants Within the required Statement on Inclusive Excellence and Belonging, candidates should include an explanation of how their teaching practice meets the needs of the diverse population of students at the University of Hartford, focusing particularly on pedagogical approaches that support student success. If relevant, candidates should also comment on research, scholarship, practice, or creative activity that will contribute to the inclusive excellence goals to which the University of Hartford is committed. It is recommended that candidates approach this statement thoughtfully and use specific examples to illustrate their Statement on Inclusive Excellence and Belonging. In your cover letter, please relate your experience to all Qualifications of the position in addition to highlighting what unique skills you would bring to The Hartt School. Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Are you currently or have you ever been a part-time faculty member at the University of Hartford? Yes No Documents Needed To Apply Required Documents Statement on Inclusive Excellence and Belonging Cover Letter Curriculum Vitae Teaching Philosophy Optional Documents 200 Bloomfield Avenue\r\nWest Hartford, CT 06117 Back to top Copyright © 2021 University of Hartford To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
